<TopicLayout title="Data Center Power Monitoring Pilot | Behind the Watt" description="A paid four-week research pilot for selected data center power projects. Review a real Abilene sample, then request a scoped watchlist and fixed-fee quote." path="/monitoring-pilot/" heading="Follow the power changes that matter to your projects" deck="A paid four-week research pilot for equipment suppliers and engineering consultants tracking US data-center power. Choose a small watchlist; receive source-linked changes and their practical meaning." kind="service" label="Monitoring pilot">
  <div slot="intro-action" class="pilot-intro-actions"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="#request" data-pilot-cta="hero">Request a scoped pilot</a><a href="/research/abilene-power-brief/">Read the Abilene sample</a></div>
  <section class="topic-section wrap">
    <div class="pilot-offer"><div><p>Bring the projects you already care about. BTW will establish a dated baseline, check the agreed public sources and explain material changes in permits, documented equipment and project milestones.</p><p>Start with up to five projects over four weeks. Coverage and a fixed fee are agreed before work begins. Requesting a scope review is free and nonbinding.</p></div><aside class="topic-note"><h2>Check the work before you inquire</h2><p>The public sample traces Abilene's equipment amendment and later operating permit, separates document dates from review dates and makes unknowns explicit.</p><p>It demonstrates the research format. It is not a client success story or a measurement of current generation.</p></aside></div>
  </section>
  <section class="topic-section wrap">
    <h2>When a project headline leaves a working question</h2>
    <p>A new MW figure can describe a campus plan, a permitted turbine cohort or an operating claim. A later amendment can change equipment counts without changing the marketing name. For a supplier or consultant, the useful question is what changed in the projects already on your list and what the evidence supports.</p>
    <p>The pilot gives a small team a repeatable research record: a baseline to work from, dated changes to assess and original documents to inspect. It can support territory planning, equipment research or a client briefing. A permit is not proof that procurement is open.</p>
    <h2>What the four-week pilot includes</h2>
    <div class="topic-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Pilot scope"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Deliverable</th><th scope="col">Agreed starting scope</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Watchlist</td><td>Up to five mutually agreed US data-center power projects, with legal entity, location and phase matched.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Initial baseline</td><td>Documented equipment, permit milestones, source dates, evidence basis and open questions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Four weekly memos</td><td>Checks of the agreed sources; material changes, evidence links, interpretation and remaining uncertainty. If no material change is found, the memo states that.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Review at the end</td><td>Which findings were useful, what remained inaccessible and whether a different watchlist or cadence would help.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fee</td><td>A fixed-fee quote after scope and source-access review. No charge, contract or automatic renewal is created by this form.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>The agreed scope defines which sources and fields are checked. This is a scheduled research service; it does not promise continuous surveillance or immediate alerts. Access restrictions and unknowns are disclosed before accepting a watchlist.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="topic-section wrap">
    <h2>From a source record to a usable memo</h2>
    <ol><li><strong>Agree the question.</strong> Identify your projects and the decision you want to support. Check whether the source coverage can answer it.</li><li><strong>Set the baseline.</strong> Keep planned, permitted, reported, observed and operating claims separate. Record the source and date for each.</li><li><strong>Explain the change.</strong> Show the earlier and later record, why the distinction matters, what is still unknown and what to verify next.</li><li><strong>Evaluate the pilot.</strong> Review the usefulness and effort before agreeing any further work.</li></ol>
    <p><a href="/research/abilene-power-brief/">The Abilene sample</a> shows the format using TCEQ 177263 and O4721. Its January 2025 turbine cohort totals 360.5 site-rated MW. The sample explains why that is different from a current delivered-power measurement.</p>
    <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="#request" data-pilot-cta="sample">Request a scoped pilot</a></p>
    <h2>Questions before starting</h2>
    <h3>Can you follow my specific projects?</h3><p>Submit the names, locations or states you need. We will check public-record availability and explain the proposed coverage before quoting. The open database is a small documented sample, not a complete US project census.</p>
    <h3>What stays free?</h3><p>Published facility dossiers, the open data and ordinary API access remain free. The pilot pays for a selected watchlist, agreed research checks and interpretation prepared for your question.</p>
    <h3>What if a source is unavailable or no change is found?</h3><p>The memo records the checks, access limits and unknowns. No-change findings and unavailable sources are stated explicitly. A missing update is not treated as evidence that construction or operation stopped.</p>
    <h3>Does this provide engineering or legal sign-off?</h3><p>The output is a source-linked research memo. Engineering design, legal compliance, procurement commitments and investment decisions require the relevant specialist assessment. Current operating output is only stated when supporting evidence exists.</p>
    <h3>Who handles the work and the inquiry?</h3><p>Yaro Korets maintains BTW and reviews pilot scope. Automated checks can assist research; the memo makes its evidence and review scope visible. Your inquiry goes to the private owner inbox for review and reply, without newsletter enrollment.</p>
  </section>
